[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one fix. The 412 reports merged into it are not
one issue. Read against the tenant directory (locale and booking form) and the report bodies,
they hold three problems. Only the first is the parser bug the ticket describes.

<Callout>
The parser fix will not stop problems 2 and 3. They have no ticket of their own until the
tickets below are filed.
</Callout>

## The three problems

| # | problem | reports | tenants | seen |
|---|---------|---------|---------|------|
| 1 | The shared parser reads every date month first, so day-first tenants get flipped dates | 374 | 11 | 2026-04-01 → 2026-10-04 |
| 2 | Booking form v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field, so people in day-first tenants type month first | 33 | 3 | 2026-04-17 → 2026-09-22 |
| 3 | An invite lands one day off, with no month flip: a time zone or day boundary problem | 5 | 5 | 2026-04-02 → 2026-10-04 |

374 + 33 + 5 = 412. Every report is counted once. The export holds 427 rows because its pages
overlap; 15 reports appear twice and are counted once.

**How this differs from the desk's cause table.** The cause table counts by tenant and channel
only, so it shows two causes: parser 379 and hint 33. It files the 5 one-day reports under the
parser and footnotes them (R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344, R-41620; the desk lists them in
its own notes). Their bodies describe a day shifting, not a month flipping: the same meeting
giving two cities two different days, an invite arriving before the booking existed, a Tuesday
showing as Monday. Counted apart, the parser group is 374, not 379. The five differ from each
other, so treat their shared cause as a hypothesis until engineering reproduces it.

## How to tell them apart

- **Look up the tenant, not the report.** Every tenant in the directory writes day first. Whether
  the cause is the parser or the form hint depends on which booking form the tenant runs and how
  the report arrived. Only a report that came through a `booking-v3` form is problem 2. The
  parser flips every other day-first report.
- **Intake's tag is a guess.** It is made from the reporter's browser. 23 reports it tagged as
  `hint` are the parser bug, and 15 it tagged as `parser` are the form hint.
- **Read the body for problem 3.** A month flip changes the month. These change only the day.

## Why the merge hid this

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports on subject line alone. The
subjects here are things like "Wrong day on the invite", which fit all three problems. Merging
on subject is fast, but it counts three bugs once and hides two of them. The reports were
merged into #4471 months before anyone read the bodies; the oldest merge in the export is dated
2026-07-02.

## What to do

1. File one ticket per problem (the tickets for this page are in the repository's issues).
2. Move the problem 2 and problem 3 reports out of #4471, so the parser fix closes only what it fixes.
3. Reopen the question in decision 0001: when two reports share a subject, check the tenant's
   locale and booking form before merging. A new record replaces 0001; 0001 is not edited.

## Lessons

- A big ticket is mostly one bug reported many times, until it isn't. Count the causes before
  you merge, not after.
- A number that can be computed should be computed. These counts come from
  `analysis/count.mjs`, which reads the export and prints them; re-run it when the export
  changes.

